# Genes as instruments: Mendelian randomization
next: {"title":"Choosing a design, and turning an estimate into a policy","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/choosing-a-design-and-turning-an-estimate-into-a-policy.cast"}

## Nature's Lottery Draw
voice: female
level: advanced
use: punnett_square
with:
    parent1: Aa
    parent2: Aa
    trait:
      dominant: carries *2 — flushes
      recessive: '*1/*1 — no flush'
    highlight: dominant
    show_ratio: true

Nobody will ever randomize you into thirty years of heavy drinking — so over four parts we ask whether a gene can stand in for the lottery we cannot run.
    text ore_title "Oregon, 2008" #2f6b8f x 265 y 560 font_size 32
    text ore_text "≈90,000 apply — 30,000 names drawn" #2f6b8f x 265 y 500 font_size 22

Oregon drew thirty thousand names from ninety thousand applicants, once, in 2008; meiosis runs its own draw at every conception, twice over.
    text nat_title "Every conception" #b5482e x 735 y 560 font_size 32
    text nat_text "2 alleles carried — 1 transmitted" #b5482e x 735 y 500 font_size 22
    path divider dashed #8f887c points [[500,610],[500,470]]

Both allocate by chance, but only one of them has been running since long before there were economists to exploit it.
    text cmp_text "Allocation by chance — once in Oregon, twice at every conception" x 500 y 400 font_size 24

    pause 0.4

    clear

Take the gene this series lives on: ALDH2 on chromosome 12, and two parents who each carry one low-activity *2 copy and one normal *1.
    draw parent1_label parent2_label grid

Write A for the *2 allele and a for *1: each parent transmits one of their two copies with probability one half, and which one is pure chance.
    draw grid__ch0 grid__ch1 grid__rh0 grid__rh1

    pause 0.3

The two transmissions are independent, so all four cells are equally likely — a two-ticket draw with no selection anywhere in it.
    draw grid__c0_0 grid__c0_1 grid__c1_0 grid__c1_1 parallel true

    pause 0.4

Three of the four inherit at least one *2 copy, letting acetaldehyde pile up after a drink — so in this lottery the losing hand is a flush.
    draw hl_0_0 hl_0_1 hl_1_0 parallel true

Three to one: Mendel's segregation ratio is the oldest randomized allocation rule in science, and it re-runs every generation.
    draw ratio

    pause 0.5

Read the conditioning carefully, though — the draw is random within these parents' genotypes, not across a population.
    focus parent1_label parent2_label

Across populations, allele frequencies track ancestry, and so do diet, income and disease; population stratification is confounding under another name, which is why credible studies condition on ancestry components or compare siblings within a family.

    clear

So what has the lottery bought us? First, independence: conditional on the parents, the allele is dealt before any covariate exists to be unbalanced.
    text head3 "What nature's lottery buys you" x 500 y 640 font_size 34
    text p1 "1. Random within parental genotype" x 500 y 545 font_size 26

Second, temporal order — the genotype is fixed at conception, decades before smoking, income or illness, so no outcome can reach back and cause it.
    text p2 "2. Fixed at conception — nothing downstream can reverse-cause it" x 500 y 460 font_size 26

Third, the part nature does not hand you: relevance, the requirement that the allele actually shifts the exposure.
    text p3 "3. Relevance is not free: the allele must move the exposure" #b5482e x 500 y 375 font_size 26
    mark mark3 target p3 kind box

In our notation Z_i is the number of *2 copies, D_i is alcohol intake, Y_i the health outcome — and the next part measures that first stage in a real population.
    text zline "Z_i = copies of *2   →   D_i = alcohol intake   →   Y_i = health" #2f6b8f x 500 y 250 font_size 26

    pause 0.4

Nature randomizes the allele; it never promised to randomize what the allele does — and that gap is the rest of this series.
    delivery grave

## Pleiotropy in a Lab Coat
voice: female
level: advanced
use: causal_dag
with:
    title: Two arrows out of one gene
    nodes:
      - {name: ALDH2 variant, role: exposure}
      - {name: Alcohol intake, role: mediator}
      - {name: Heart disease, role: outcome}
      - {name: Aldehyde toxicity, role: collider}
      - {name: Ancestry, role: confounder}
      - {name: Family environment, role: confounder}
    edges: ALDH2 variant -> Alcohol intake; Alcohol intake -> Heart disease; ALDH2 variant -> Aldehyde toxicity; Aldehyde toxicity -> Heart disease; Ancestry -> ALDH2 variant; Ancestry -> Heart disease; Family environment -> ALDH2 variant; Family environment -> Heart disease
    highlight_backdoor: true

    mark mark_horizontal target node_aldehyde_toxicity kind circle hidden true

Now that the ALDH2 flush has given us a genuinely strong first stage, the question that decides whether we may publish a number at all is whether this gene touches the heart by any route other than the glass.
    draw title node_aldh2_variant

One copy of the star-two allele lets acetaldehyde accumulate, drinking turns unpleasant, and intake falls — to near zero among homozygotes.
    draw node_alcohol_intake edge_0

    pause 0.3

Alcohol then acts on blood pressure and the heart: this arrow is the effect we actually want.
    draw node_heart_disease edge_1

Strictly in series — that is vertical pleiotropy, and far from a violation it is the instrument doing its job.
    focus node_aldh2_variant node_alcohol_intake node_heart_disease edge_0 edge_1

    pause 0.4

But the same variant leaves acetaldehyde itself circulating, and acetaldehyde is a group-one carcinogen and a vasodilator in its own right.
    draw node_aldehyde_toxicity edge_2

A second arrow reaches the outcome without ever passing through drinking — horizontal pleiotropy, which is our exclusion restriction from lecture seven wearing a lab coat.
    draw edge_3 mark_horizontal delivery grave

And the damage is arithmetic: the Wald ratio converges not to beta but to beta plus gamma over pi — direct effect over first stage. Bias you inherit.
    text wald "plim β̂_IV = β + γ / π" #b5482e x 500 y 58 font_size 30

    pause 0.5

Randomization at conception holds within a mating pool, not across them: ancestry moves allele frequencies and disease risk together — pool populations carelessly and you can discover a gene for chopstick use.
    draw node_ancestry edge_4 edge_5

Dynastic effects next: your genotype is correlated with your parents', and their genotype built the household that acted on you directly.
    draw node_family_environment edge_6 edge_7

Both are omitted-variable bias in genetic dress — they break independence before exclusion is even reached, which is why credible work adjusts for ancestry components or moves inside families.
    focus node_ancestry node_family_environment edge_4 edge_5 edge_6 edge_7

    pause 0.5

    clear keep ["title"]

Modern Mendelian randomization rarely rests on one variant: a polygenic score aggregates hundreds of SNPs, and the first stage becomes so strong that lecture eight's weak-instrument worry simply evaporates.
    text t_head "Polygenic score: hundreds of SNPs, one instrument" x 500 y 600 font_size 30
    text t_f "First stage: F in the hundreds" #2f6b8f x 500 y 520 font_size 26

Which is the trap — a score selected for prediction is selected precisely for touching everything it can, so strength is bought with hundreds of new direct paths.
    text t_cost "Exclusion: hundreds of ways to fail" #b5482e x 500 y 462 font_size 26

So exclusion gets tested rather than assumed: MR-Egger reads a nonzero intercept as directional pleiotropy, and the weighted median survives if over half the instrument weight is valid.
    text t_m1 "MR-Egger — nonzero intercept ⇒ directional pleiotropy" x 500 y 372 font_size 24
    text t_m2 "Weighted median — consistent if >50% of weight is valid" x 500 y 316 font_size 24

Within-sibship designs difference out ancestry and the family home — the one setting where comparing yourself with your sibling is scientifically productive.
    text t_m3 "Within-sibship MR — differences out ancestry and home" x 500 y 260 font_size 24

Each carries assumptions of its own, and whether a polygenic score can ever satisfy exclusion remains openly disputed in the field.
    text t_bottom "Falsification tests, not proof." #b5482e x 500 y 160 font_size 30
    mark mark_bottom target t_bottom kind box

Everything hangs on one arrow leaving the gene rather than two: Mendelian randomization does not escape the exclusion restriction, it relocates it into biology, where it is harder to see and no easier to test.
    show node_aldh2_variant node_alcohol_intake node_heart_disease node_aldehyde_toxicity edge_0 edge_1 edge_2 edge_3 mark_horizontal delivery grave

## What the gene can and cannot tell you
voice: female
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,70],"y":[0,25]}

    path grid_h #8f887c points [[60,612],[960,612]] hidden true
    path grid_v1 #8f887c points [[335,225],[335,618]] hidden true
    path grid_v2 #8f887c points [[672,225],[672,618]] hidden true
    text t1 "One checklist, two lotteries" x 500 y 700 font_size 30 hidden true
    text head_1 "Oregon lottery" #2f6b8f x 500 y 648 font_size 23 hidden true
    text head_2 "ALDH2 rs671" #b5482e x 816 y 648 font_size 23 hidden true
    text r1 "Relevance" x 195 y 555 font_size 22 hidden true
    text c11 "+25 pp coverage" #2f6b8f x 500 y 555 font_size 19 hidden true
    text c12 "≈ half the drinks/week" #b5482e x 816 y 555 font_size 19 hidden true
    text r2 "Independence" x 195 y 460 font_size 22 hidden true
    text c21 "A state computer, 2008" #2f6b8f x 500 y 460 font_size 19 hidden true
    text c22 "Meiosis, at conception" #b5482e x 816 y 460 font_size 19 hidden true
    text r3 "Exclusion" x 195 y 365 font_size 22 hidden true
    text c31 "Letter changes nothing else" #2f6b8f x 500 y 365 font_size 19 hidden true
    text c32 "No horizontal pleiotropy" #b5482e x 816 y 365 font_size 19 hidden true
    mark mark_excl target c32 kind box hidden true

Now that we have seen pleiotropy break the exclusion restriction, the question a ministry will actually ask you is whether an MR estimate can be taken to a policy — so let's hold the gene and the Oregon lottery to the same four-item checklist.
    draw grid_h grid_v1 grid_v2 t1 head_1 head_2

Relevance: the Oregon draw raised Medicaid coverage by about twenty-five percentage points, and one copy of the rs671 variant roughly halves weekly drinking — both first stages are strong.
    draw r1 c11 c12

    pause 0.3

Independence: Oregon's assignment came from a state computer in 2008, the gene's from meiosis at conception — and nobody chose whether to enter that second draw.
    draw r2 c21 c22

    pause 0.3

Exclusion: the letter must move health only through coverage, the variant only through alcohol — and this is the door pleiotropy walks through.
    draw r3 c31 c32

    pause 0.3

Monotonicity: nobody enrolled in Medicaid because they lost the lottery, and nobody drinks more because their face turns red — no defiers on either side.
    text r4 "Monotonicity" x 195 y 270 font_size 22
    text c41 "No one enrolls on losing" #2f6b8f x 500 y 270 font_size 19
    text c42 "Flush never raises drinking" #b5482e x 816 y 270 font_size 19

    pause 0.5

Three of these four rungs the gene climbs more easily than the lottery does; this one it can never climb, only argue.
    draw mark_excl delivery grave

    clear

    axes ax x_label "Age (years)" y_label "Drinks per week"

Take two men of the same cohort: the one with two functional alleles drinks more through his twenties and settles near twenty units a week.
    curve curve_wt #2f6b8f expr 20/(1+exp(-(x-22)/2.5))
    label label_wt "Two functional alleles" above #2f6b8f attach_to curve_wt

    pause 0.3

His neighbour carries the variant, flushes on the second drink, and settles at about half that — from his first drink to his last.
    curve curve_het #b5482e expr 10/(1+exp(-(x-22)/2.5))
    label label_het "Carries rs671: flushes" below #b5482e attach_to curve_het

    pause 0.3

So the instrument's contrast is this entire slab: four decades at half the dose, not one year of it.
    region region_gap x_from 0 x_to 70 between ["curve_wt","curve_het"] style.fill #f2c14e
    label label_gap "Lifetime exposure contrast" above-left attach_to region_gap

Run exactly this contrast in the China Kadoorie Biobank — half a million adults — and stroke risk climbs steadily with genotype-predicted alcohol, with no protective sweet spot for moderate drinking.
    highlight region_gap effect glow

    pause 0.4

Now the object a finance ministry actually controls: a tax that shaves five units a week off midlife drinking for five years — this thin green sliver.
    curve curve_policy dashed #d0865f expr "20/(1+exp(-(x-22)/2.5)) - 5*exp(-((x-45)*(x-45))/12)"
    region region_policy x_from 38 x_to 52 between ["curve_wt","curve_policy"] style.fill #87a878
    label label_policy "Five-year alcohol tax" below-right #d0865f attach_to region_policy

The gene's local average treatment effect is anything but late: it switches on before the first drink and never switches off.
    point at.ref region_gap gesture circle

    clear

What the gene can give you is the sign and the scale of lifetime exposure, uncontaminated by reverse causation or by the habits that travel with drinking.
    text syn_title "What the gene can and cannot tell you" x 500 y 590 font_size 30
    text s1 "Can: the sign and scale of lifetime exposure" #2f6b8f x 500 y 480 font_size 24

What it cannot give you is a policy effect — rescaling that lifetime slab down to a five-year sliver is extrapolation, not estimation.
    text s2 "Cannot: the effect of any policy you could pass" #b5482e x 500 y 400 font_size 24

And what it always gives you is a LATE, defined by compliers you never chose — the heterozygotes the flush actually moves, just as Oregon's estimate belonged to the quarter who enrolled.
    text s3 "Always: a LATE, on compliers you did not choose" x 500 y 320 font_size 24
    mark mark_s3 target s3 kind box

    pause 0.4

Nature randomizes the allele for free, and that buys independence outright; exclusion and the complier definition you still pay for — in biology, and in how far you dare carry the number.

